# Rules for every fixture worker (W2 fs, W3 db, W4 recovery)

Read `fixtures/base.py` in full first: it is the contract. Do not edit it. Read
`BENCHMARK_BRIEF.md` §5 for the idea. Use `.venv/bin/python`.

## What one fixture module looks like
`fixtures/<family>/<id>.py` defines `PAIR = Pair(...)` with 2 variants ("A", "B").
- `build(root)` creates everything with Python (`pathlib`, `os.symlink`, `os.link`, `sqlite3`) or
  `sh(root, ...)`. git repos: `sh(root, "git init -q -b main && git add -A && git commit -qm init")`
  (env is pinned by `sh`).
- `artifacts(root)` returns real command output from the built env, via `sh(root, cmd).stdout`.
  Allowed commands: `ls -la <dir>`, `ls -laR`, `cat <file>`, `git status --short`,
  `git ls-files`, `sqlite3 app.db .schema`, `sqlite3 -header -column app.db "SELECT ..."`,
  `stat -c '%n %F links=%h' <paths>` (GNU form; for links use `ls -la`, which shows `-> target`).
  NO inode numbers (`ls -i`), no sizes in blocks, no timestamps other than the fixed mtime the
  harness sets (`ls -la` dates are fine: they will all read Jan 1 2026).
  Prefer `ls -la` over `ls -l` so hard-link counts and symlink targets are visible.
- The artifact title is the command line shown, e.g. `$ ls -la uploads` or `$ cat config/cleanup.env`.
- Every variant returns the SAME titles in the SAME order. Bodies should be similar in length.
- `operation` and `restore_script` are bash, shown verbatim and executed verbatim. Keep each under
  ~6 lines. Restore scripts must never abort on a missing source: guard with `[ -f x ] && ... || true`.
- Inventory: 6–10 resources, IDs R1..Rn, identical across variants. Include decoys that survive in
  both variants. Labels say exactly what is fingerprinted ("contents of X", "symlink X",
  "contents of dir/", "orders rows 101-104"). Shuffle so the interesting resource is not always R1.
- `literal_targets`: IDs the operation names directly (glob targets count when the glob is literal,
  e.g. `rm -rf uploads/*` targets everything under uploads/).
- `hops`: number of artifacts that must be combined to get the answer right.
- `expected`: your hypothesis for each variant ({"A": {"lost": [...], "recoverable": [...]}, ...}).
  The harness checks it against execution. If your test shows a mismatch, fix the fixture or the
  hypothesis and SAY SO in your report; never fudge a fingerprint to make it match.

## Surface-tell rules (reviewers will attack these)
- The variant letter, the word "typo", "bug", "danger", "careful", comments that explain the trap,
  and names like `old_`, `broken_`, `tmp_bad` are forbidden.
- If the bad variant contains a distinctive token (symlink, NULL, .gitignore, trigger, .snapshots,
  CASCADE, AUTO_SYNC=1, empty file), the good variant must contain the same kind of token harmlessly
  (an unrelated symlink, a NULL in an irrelevant column, a .gitignore for a different path, a trigger
  on a different table, ...). Balance A and B: sometimes A is the "nothing lost" one, sometimes B.
- Synthetic, boring, realistic content: small CSVs, notes, configs. No real people or companies.

## Tests
Write `tests/test_fixtures_<family>.py`: for each pair and variant, build in `tmp_path`, apply fixed
mtimes is NOT needed, run operation and restore via `subprocess.run(["bash","--noprofile","--norc","-c", ...],
cwd=root, env=env_for(root))`, compute lost/recoverable from the inventory fingerprints exactly as
`fixtures/base.py` describes, and assert they equal `expected`. Also assert: same artifact titles
across variants; every fingerprint non-None before the operation; no forbidden words in any artifact.
You are on macOS (bash 3.2, BSD coreutils): if a GNU-only behaviour makes a test fail locally,
mark that single test `pytest.mark.skipif(sys.platform == "darwin", reason=...)` and explain it
in your report. Final gold is produced on Debian bookworm (bash 5.2, GNU coreutils 9.1, git 2.39,
sqlite 3.40); design for that.

## Report
Files created, pytest output, every place you deviated from the brief and why, and any pair where
you are not sure the expected outcome is right.
"""Shared contract for every fixture. Workers code against this file; do not change it.

A fixture module (fixtures/<family>/<pair>.py, e.g. fixtures/fs/fs1.py) exposes:

    PAIR = Pair(...)          # one matched pair (2 or 3 variants)

The harness (harness/run.py) does, per variant, in a fresh temp dir `root`:
    variant.build(root)                    # create files / git repo / sqlite db
    set_fixed_mtimes(root)                 # harness does this, fixtures don't
    artifacts = variant.artifacts(root)    # rendered from the BUILT env (real ls/cat/schema output)
    before = fingerprints(root)
    result = run_operation(root, variant.operation, variant.op_kind)
    after = fingerprints(root)             # lost = fingerprint changed or became None
    run(root, pair.restore_script)         # the exact script shown to the model
    restored = fingerprints(root)          # recoverable = lost and restored == before

Gold therefore means: "lost by this command" and "brought back exactly by THIS restore script".
It does not claim that no other recovery method exists; the prompt says so.

Resource identity (pick the fingerprint that matches the label, and say it in the label):
- file_fp:   the bytes readable at a path (follows symlinks). Label: "contents of reports/q3.csv".
- link_fp:   a symlink object itself (its target text), never its target. Label: "symlink backup/q3.csv".
- tree_fp:   a directory's contents. Label: "contents of uploads/archive/".
- sqlite_fp: specific PRE-EXISTING rows selected by key, ORDER BY primary key. Label:
             "orders rows 101-104 (customer 7)". Never a whole table: inserts elsewhere (archive
             triggers, audit rows) must not count as a loss. Only pre-existing state is inventoried.

Rules:
- `operation` is the exact text shown to the model and the exact text executed.
- Resource IDs and labels must be identical across the variants of a pair (same inventory).
  Only the environment differs, in the one fact named by `changed_fact`.
- Everything that decides the outcome must appear in `artifacts`.
- Nothing may depend on wall-clock time, randomness, inode numbers, $USER or the host.
- Synthetic content only.
- Surface tells: both variants show the same artifact titles in the same order, with bodies of
  similar length. A "scary" token that marks the bad variant (symlink, NULL, .gitignore, trigger,
  snapshot, checksum, AUTO_SYNC, ...) must also appear harmlessly in the other variant.
- Shell ops run with a clean env (`env -i`-style), no startup files, default shopt (no dotglob,
  nullglob, failglob, nounset unless the shown script sets them).
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import hashlib
import os
import sqlite3
import subprocess
from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Callable, List, Optional

FIXED_EPOCH = 1767225600  # 2026-01-01T00:00:00Z, used for every mtime and git date

# Environment for every subprocess the fixtures and harness run. No host leakage.
BASE_ENV = {
    "PATH": "/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin",
    "LC_ALL": "C",
    "LANG": "C",
    "TZ": "UTC",
    "GIT_AUTHOR_NAME": "Dev Example",
    "GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL": "dev@example.invalid",
    "GIT_COMMITTER_NAME": "Dev Example",
    "GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL": "dev@example.invalid",
    "GIT_AUTHOR_DATE": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
    "GIT_COMMITTER_DATE": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
    "GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM": "1",
    "GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS": "0",  # `git status` in artifacts must not rewrite .git/index (mtime leak)
}


def home_for(root: Path) -> Path:
    """HOME lives next to root (not inside it), so it never shows up in listings or globs."""
    home = root.parent / (root.name + ".home")
    home.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
    return home


def env_for(root: Path) -> dict:
    """Subprocess env with HOME pinned beside the sandbox root."""
    env = dict(BASE_ENV)
    env["HOME"] = str(home_for(root))
    env["GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL"] = str(home_for(root) / ".gitconfig")
    return env


def sh(root: Path, script: str, check: bool = True) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    """Run a bash script with cwd=root and the pinned env. Used by build/restore/artifacts."""
    return subprocess.run(
        ["bash", "-c", script], cwd=str(root), env=env_for(root),
        capture_output=True, text=True, check=check, timeout=60,
    )


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- fingerprints

def file_fp(rel: str) -> Callable[[Path], Optional[str]]:
    """Content hash of one regular file (follows symlinks). None if missing/dangling/not a file."""
    def fp(root: Path) -> Optional[str]:
        p = root / rel
        if not p.is_file():
            return None
        return hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest()
    return fp


def link_fp(rel: str) -> Callable[[Path], Optional[str]]:
    """The symlink object at `rel` (its target text). None if absent or not a symlink."""
    def fp(root: Path) -> Optional[str]:
        p = root / rel
        if not p.is_symlink():
            return None
        return "link:" + os.readlink(p)
    return fp


def tree_fp(rel: str) -> Callable[[Path], Optional[str]]:
    """Hash typed, length-prefixed entries without following symlinked directories."""
    def fp(root: Path) -> Optional[str]:
        base = root / rel
        if not base.is_dir() or base.is_symlink():
            return None
        h = hashlib.sha256()
        entries = []
        for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(base):
            for name in dirnames + filenames:
                p = Path(dirpath) / name
                if p.is_symlink():
                    kind, payload = b"link", os.fsencode(os.readlink(p))
                elif p.is_dir():
                    kind, payload = b"dir", b""
                else:
                    kind, payload = b"file", p.read_bytes()
                entries.append((os.fsencode(p.relative_to(base)), kind, payload))
            dirnames[:] = sorted(name for name in dirnames
                                 if not (Path(dirpath) / name).is_symlink())
        for path, kind, payload in sorted(entries):
            h.update(kind + b"|" + len(path).to_bytes(8, "big") + b"|" + path
                     + b"|" + len(payload).to_bytes(8, "big") + b"|" + payload)
        return h.hexdigest()
    return fp


def sqlite_fp(db_rel: str, query: str) -> Callable[[Path], Optional[str]]:
    """Hash of the rows returned by `query` (ordered by the query itself). None if the db or
    table is missing or the query returns no rows."""
    def fp(root: Path) -> Optional[str]:
        p = root / db_rel
        if not p.is_file():
            return None
        con = sqlite3.connect(str(p))
        try:
            rows = con.execute(query).fetchall()
        except sqlite3.Error:
            return None
        finally:
            con.close()
        if not rows:
            return None
        return hashlib.sha256(repr(rows).encode()).hexdigest()
    return fp


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- data shapes

@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Resource:
    id: str      # "R1", "R2", ... unique within the pair
    label: str   # what the model sees, e.g. "file data/customers.csv" or "orders rows for customer 7"
    fingerprint: Callable[[Path], Optional[str]]


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Artifact:
    title: str   # e.g. "$ ls -la" or "config/cleanup.env" or "sqlite3 app.db .schema"
    body: str    # exact text; produced from the built environment, not hand-typed


@dataclass
class Variant:
    variant: str                                  # "A", "B", optionally "C" (never shown to the model)
    build: Callable[[Path], None]                 # create the environment under root
    artifacts: Callable[[Path], List[Artifact]]   # render what the model sees
    hops: int                                     # artifacts that must be combined (1..3)


@dataclass
class Pair:
    pair_id: str                 # "FS-1"
    family: str                  # "fs" | "db" | "recovery"
    changed_fact: str            # one line, for analysis only (never shown to the model)
    operation: str               # exact bash shown to the model and run as `bash -c` with cwd=root.
                                 # SQL ops: sqlite3 -bail app.db "PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON; BEGIN; ...; COMMIT;"
                                 # (-bail stops at the first error; the open transaction is rolled back
                                 # when sqlite3 exits). Wrapper scripts are fine if built into root and
                                 # their source is shown as an artifact.
    restore_script: str          # exact bash shown to the model and executed after the operation.
                                 # May only use what survived; must not fail loudly when a source is
                                 # missing (use `|| true` / guards), so restore errors never decide gold.
    inventory: List[Resource]
    literal_targets: List[str]   # IDs the command names literally (for the frozen naive-reader baseline),
                                 # e.g. `rm reports/q3.csv` -> the resource for reports/q3.csv.
    variants: List[Variant] = field(default_factory=list)
    expected: dict = field(default_factory=dict)  # {"A": {"lost": [...], "recoverable": [...]}, ...}
    # `expected` is the author's hypothesis. The harness reports any mismatch with the executed
    # result; executed results are the gold, never this dict.
"""Render the exact, label-free question given to a model."""

from fixtures.base import Artifact, Pair


def render_prompt(pair: Pair, variant_artifacts: list[Artifact], tool_versions: dict[str, str]) -> str:
    versions = tool_versions
    parts = [
        "You are reviewing a command before anyone runs it. Do not run anything. Predict the outcome.",
        "",
        f"Environment: Linux, {versions['bash']}, {versions['coreutils']}, {versions['git']}, SQLite {versions['sqlite']}. The command runs as a non-root user in a fresh bash process (no startup files, default shell options, a clean environment where only PATH, HOME, LANG/LC_ALL=C and TZ=UTC are set) with the project root below as working directory.",
        "The restore script then runs the same way, in a new bash process, from the project root.",
        "",
    ]
    for artifact in variant_artifacts:
        parts.extend((f"=== {artifact.title} ===", artifact.body.rstrip("\n"), ""))
    parts.extend((
        "=== Command to be run ===", pair.operation, "",
        "=== Restore script (run afterwards in a new bash process) ===",
        pair.restore_script, "",
        "=== Resources ===",
    ))
    parts.extend(f"{resource.id}: {resource.label}" for resource in pair.inventory)
    parts.extend((
        "", "Answer with JSON only, no prose:",
        '{"lost": [...], "recoverable": [...], "permanent_loss": true|false}',
        '- "lost": IDs of resources whose content is gone or changed right after the command.',
        '- "recoverable": the subset of "lost" that the restore script above brings back with exactly the',
        '  original content. Judge only this script, not other ways you might recover data.',
        '- "permanent_loss": true if any lost resource is not recoverable.',
        'Use only the resource IDs listed above, written as strings exactly as listed (for example "R2", not 2).',
        'Use [] for an empty list. Example of the format only (not a hint about this case):',
        '{"lost": ["R1", "R4"], "recoverable": ["R4"], "permanent_loss": true}',
    ))
    return "\n".join(parts) + "\n"
**The main validity blockers are missing evidence for exact restoration.** Executed gold agrees with the builders, but several prompts permit different answers.

- **FS-2B — breaks:** [fs2.py](/Users/sjh/Dev.to%20Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/fs/fs2.py) shows archive filenames, not whether archived `2024.csv` equals the original. R5’s exact recovery is therefore unproven. **Fix:** render hashes of the live file and extracted archive member. The dangling-symlink `unlink` line **is present** in both prompts; that suspected omission is resolved.
- **FS-3A — breaks:** [fs3.py](/Users/sjh/Dev.to%20Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/fs/fs3.py) shows matching sizes and link counts, which do not establish that the two `q3.csv` paths share an inode or identical bytes. Different 19-byte files satisfy the listing but invalidate recovery. **Fix:** show content hashes or explicit shared-file identity.
- **R-2A/B — breaks:** [r2.py](/Users/sjh/Dev.to%20Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/recovery/r2.py) displays a standalone `MANIFEST`; nothing establishes that it describes the actual tar members. **Fix:** generate the displayed listing and hashes directly from archive contents.
- **R-3A — breaks:** [r3.py](/Users/sjh/Dev.to%20Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/recovery/r3.py) establishes nonzero archive size and says the job writes every segment, but omits member paths and equality with current bytes. An older complete archive fits. **Fix:** display archive members and matching hashes.
- **FS-1B — breaks:** the clean environment/default shell options in `fixtures/base.py` never reach the prompt. An inherited `SUBDIR=tmp` defeats the intended typo outcome. **Fix:** render the clean-environment, unset-variable and default-globbing assumptions.
- **R-4 — weakens:** [r4.py](/Users/sjh/Dev.to%20Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/recovery/r4.py) omits database schemas. The displayed rows alone cannot exclude triggers or constraints affecting deletion/restoration. **Fix:** include both schemas.

Every pair has a shallow discriminator. “Worse” below means more resources remain unrestored; DB-2, FS-4, R-2 and R-3 have identical immediate losses across variants. Paths identify fixture files.

| Pair/file | Cue predicting worse variant | Severity; concrete fixture fix |
|---|---|---|
| FS-1 `fs/fs1.py` | B alone has `SUBDR`; one character shorter. | **Weakens:** include both variable names in both variants; vary which assignment is sourced. |
| FS-2 `fs/fs2.py` | B’s first listing says `total 20`; restore explicitly anticipates a dangling `store/archive`. | **Weakens:** normalize irrelevant listing fields; counterbalance link direction and deletion target across examples. |
| FS-3 `fs/fs3.py` | B lacks the unrelated `latest.csv -> q2.csv`; first total is 24 versus 20. | **Weakens:** keep unrelated links identical; remove allocation totals. |
| FS-4 `fs/fs4.py` | B has `!! data/`, larger `.gitignore`, and one fewer tracked filename. | **Weakens:** counterbalance ignored/tracked distractors and vary the deleted resource. |
| DB-1 `db/db1.py` | A lacks `RESTRICT`; B uniquely contains it. | **Weakens:** place RESTRICT on an unrelated relation in A; counterbalance which relation deletion reaches. |
| DB-2 `db/db2.py` | B has `OLD.method`; A has `OLD.status`. Resource wording directly identifies void invoices. | **Weakens:** include both trigger bodies and neutral key-based labels; vary the invoked table. |
| DB-3 `db/db3.py` | B has `actor TEXT NOT NULL`; A is worse. | **Weakens:** counterbalance nullable values and constrained columns across fixtures. |
| DB-4 `db/db4.py` | B uniquely contains `2026-02-03`; A is worse. “Active users” marks protected R1. | **Weakens:** keep unrelated dates constant; label R1 `users rows 2,5`. |
| R-1 `recovery/r1.py` | B alone contains unrelated `config.tar`; A alone has `wal-old.tar`. | **Weakens:** retain identical distractor archives in both variants. |
| R-2 `recovery/r2.py` | B alone has `orders.seg 40`; no checksum comparison needed. | **Weakens:** include equal-length changed-content backups and harmless count mismatches. |
| R-3 `recovery/r3.py` | B’s backup listing says `total 8`; shorter padding also identifies it. | **Weakens:** remove totals; add nonempty archives that cannot restore the target. |
| R-4 `recovery/r4.py` | `AUTO_SYNC=1` always means B/worse. | **Weakens:** counterbalance copy direction/timing so enabled synchronization sometimes preserves recovery. |

**A prompt-only shortcut beats the frozen baseline.** Default to `lost=recoverable=[]`. When the command contains `rm -rf volumes/pgdata`, set both to `{R2,R4,R6}`, then apply:

- `config.tar` present: lost `{R2,R3,R4,R6}`, recoverable empty.
- Otherwise `pgdata/orders.seg 40` present: recoverable `{R4,R6}`.
- Otherwise `total 8` present: recoverable empty.

Compute the Boolean from those sets. Hand tally: all six R-1–R-3 items, plus DB-1B/3B/4B: **9/24 items, 3/12 pairs (25%)**. The naive-reader gets DB-2B, FS-2A, FS-3A, R-2A, R-3A: **5/24 items, 0/12 pairs**. This is a post-hoc vulnerability demonstration, not a generalization estimate. Fix the repeated `{R2,R4,R6}` placement by independently permuting inventory IDs across pairs.

**Fingerprint wording — weakens:** explicitly define file resources as bytes readable *at the named path, following symlinks*, and directory resources as recursive names/content with metadata excluded. DB-4’s “active” is misleading given ended sessions; its key-based gold itself is sound.

Read-only inspection and hand scoring; no files changed. **Verdict: harden before freezing; current pilot scores cannot establish semantic tracing.****Findings**
- [W10-heldout-recovery.md](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/.agent/tasks/W10-heldout-recovery.md:18>): `cp --update=none` is not a Debian bookworm/coreutils 9.1 interface; bookworm `cp(1)` only documents `-u, --update` as “copy only when SOURCE is newer or missing” ([Debian manpage](https://manpages.debian.org/bookworm/coreutils/cp.1.en.html)). Fix: remove `cp --update=none`; require `cp -n` only, and state expected exit behavior.

- [W8-heldout-fs.md](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/.agent/tasks/W8-heldout-fs.md:18>): `mv src dst` into an existing directory is described as “nothing lost.” Under this benchmark, resources are path/content fingerprints, so `src` disappears and `dst/` tree changes. Fix: say “destination regular file is not overwritten” instead; tell workers not to inventory `src` as “not lost” unless restore moves it back.

- [W9-heldout-db.md](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/.agent/tasks/W9-heldout-db.md:17>): subquery name resolution only deletes every row if `purge_queue` is non-empty. If empty, it deletes none. Fix: require the artifact to show at least one purge row, or word the mechanism as “every row when the inner table has rows.”

- [W10-heldout-recovery.md](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/.agent/tasks/W10-heldout-recovery.md:8>) and [W10-heldout-recovery.md](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/.agent/tasks/W10-heldout-recovery.md:28>): `tar -tvf`/`tar -tf` alone repeats the pilot’s “manifest proves names, not bytes” weakness. Exact recovery can still be disputed. Fix: require per-member archive hashes generated from the archive plus live hashes for inventoried resources whenever recovery claims byte equality.

- [W8-heldout-fs.md](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/.agent/tasks/W8-heldout-fs.md:26>): `xargs` “spaces or quotes” is too broad. GNU `xargs` treats quotes/backslashes as syntax; unmatched quotes can make the command fail rather than delete a predictable filename set. Fix: restrict to spaces only, or prescribe exact filenames and expected GNU `xargs` diagnostics.

- [heldout-common.md](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/.agent/tasks/heldout-common.md:50>): “A/B differ in at most 4 lines” plus “one small fact” still permits token classifiers if each mechanism has a stable polarity. Fix: require every distinctive semantic token to appear in at least one harmless counterexample across that worker’s 8 pairs, and require no repeated full gold triple, not just no repeated lost-ID set.

- [heldout-common.md](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/.agent/tasks/heldout-common.md:21>): 8 pairs per worker, 3–5 artifacts, one 30+ line artifact, tests, and platform notes is not realistic in ~1 hour, especially DB/recovery. Fix: split each family across two workers or cut to 4–5 pairs per worker and let orchestrator top up only after review.

- [heldout-common.md](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/.agent/tasks/heldout-common.md:55>) + [W9-heldout-db.md](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/.agent/tasks/W9-heldout-db.md:16>): workers are told to verify SQLite/GNU behavior but also not run Docker, while local SQLite may differ. Fix: provide a Debian probe script/output for the risky mechanisms, or make orchestrator verification a pre-freeze blocker before any model run.

Read-only review; no files changed. **Verdict: do not launch workers unchanged.** The set is close, but fix the bookworm `cp` issue, tighten archive proof, and reduce/split the workload before freezing gold.**Revise before dispatch. The main risks are disputed gold and padding that passes the hardness checks without requiring deeper reasoning.**

1. **Wrong or underspecified mechanisms**

- [W10:18](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/.agent/tasks/W10-heldout-recovery.md:18>): `cp --update=none` is unsupported in coreutils 9.1; it fails instead of implementing no-clobber. **Fix:** require `cp -n`. [Bookworm manual](https://manpages.debian.org/bookworm/coreutils/cp.1.en.html).

- [W8:18](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/.agent/tasks/W8-heldout-fs.md:18>): `mv src dst_directory` does **not** mean “nothing lost” under `file_fp`: bytes disappear from the inventoried `src` path. It can also overwrite `dst/src`. **Fix:** specify inventory paths and destination contents explicitly; count relocation as loss at the original path. Likewise, menu 10 requires `dst` to be a **directory**, not merely existing.

- **W8 menu 8:** `set -e` is not ignored everywhere in `&&`/`||` lists: the final command is exempt from that suppression. `true && f` differs from `f && true`. **Fix:** provide exact contrasting call sites, not “an && context.” [Bash manual](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/bash.html).

- [W9:17](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/.agent/tasks/W9-heldout-db.md:17>): correlated `IN` deletes “every row” only with a nonempty queue and non-NULL outer IDs. Menu 6 also overgeneralizes: errors using `ROLLBACK` can end the transaction, while `FAIL` can preserve partial statement changes. **Fix:** require nonempty queue/non-NULL keys and a specifically chosen default-ABORT constraint failure. Explicitly set connection-local FK pragmas in every relevant wrapper/restore. [SQLite conflict semantics](https://www.sqlite.org/lang_conflict.html).

- **W10 menus 2 and 9:** the rotation pipeline emits basenames but runs from project root, so `xargs rm` targets the wrong directory. Symlink-directed restoration depends on the exact tool: GNU tar can replace directory symlinks; moreover, writing through a symlink can successfully restore `file_fp` at that path. **Fix:** show `cd backups` or prefixed paths; specify extraction options/member types and fingerprint the intended paths. [GNU tar options](https://www.gnu.org/s/tar/manual/html_node/Option-Summary.html).

2. **Rules still allowing ambiguity and shortcuts**

- **W10 evidence rule, lines 8–10:** `tar -tvf` proves names/types/sizes, **not original bytes**. Menu 7’s link count 2 does not prove that the displayed snapshot and source share an inode. These repeat the pilot’s validity failures. **Fix:** require archive-derived hashes matched against live bytes, and explicit generated same-file evidence for hard links.

- [heldout-common:19–50](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/.agent/tasks/heldout-common.md:19>): workers can declare `hops=3`, pad a listing to 30 lines, and retain one decisive cue. Harmless token duplication and A/B balance do not defeat “inspect only the operational token; ignore distractors.” R1 avoidance merely moves the positional bias. **Fix:** require workers to identify the necessary fact in each claimed artifact; audit whether the answer remains inferable without it. Distribute decisive IDs across all positions and counterbalance cue→outcome relationships.

3. **One-hour feasibility traps**

- **heldout-common:21–30, 50:** a deciding line inside a ≤25-line wrapper cannot also sit inside the required 30-line artifact without additional presentation machinery. Four rendered-line differences can also be exceeded by legitimate consequences of one fact: schema headers, listings, archive prefixes and hashes. **Fix:** separate the long-context requirement from deciding-line placement; permit documented consequential output differences.

- **heldout-common:9, 53–69:** workers must verify GNU semantics by execution, cannot run Docker, and may skip incompatible macOS tests. This transfers the hardest validation to integration immediately before the deadline. **Fix:** preflight selected mechanisms on bookworm before dispatch and supply a shared test helper. Budget six verified pairs per worker first—18 satisfies PLAN—with the remaining six conditional on time.

Read-only review; no files changed. In-memory SQLite probes supported the affinity example and exposed the correlated-query preconditions, but used local SQLite 3.53.4, not target 3.40.

**Verdict: revise briefs before workers start; the current rules cannot substantiate either unique gold or greater difficulty.****Revise before launching workers. The main risks are disputed gold and rules that reward padding.**

- **W10 menu #3 — wrong version:** `cp --update=none` requires coreutils 9.3; bookworm’s 9.1 rejects it. **Fix:** offer only `cp -n`. [GNU release notes](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/coreutils/coreutils/v9.3/NEWS).

- **W8 menu #5 — wrong under this contract:** moving `src` into a directory still loses an inventoried `file_fp("src")`; bytes elsewhere do not preserve that resource. **Fix:** explicitly count source-path loss and distinguish destination overwrite.

- **W8 menu #3/#8/#10 — missing conditions:** untracked files obstructing tracked paths can be removed by `reset --hard`; `errexit` is not suppressed for the final command in an `&&`/`||` list; `cp -r` nesting requires an existing **directory**, not merely an existing destination. **Fix:** state those preconditions. [Git reset](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-reset), [Bash manual](https://www.gnu.org/s/bash/manual/bash.html).

- **W10 menu #2/#9 — misleading templates:** the shown rotation pipeline passes bare filenames to `rm` from project root, so it misses `backups/`. Symlink redirection depends on the restore command: tar normally replaces an existing symlink when extracting the corresponding directory member. **Fix:** rotate inside `backups`; specify the exact symlink traversal mechanism and archive member types. [GNU tar](https://www.gnu.org/s/tar/manual/html_node/Option-Summary.html).

- **W9 menu #1/#6 — overclaims:** correlated `id` deletes everything only with a nonempty queue and non-NULL outer IDs. Continuing after an error does not always “skip” the statement: `FAIL` can preserve partial changes, while `ROLLBACK` ends the transaction. **Fix:** show those data preconditions and restrict the error fixture to default `ABORT`. [SQLite expressions](https://www.sqlite.org/lang_expr.html), [conflict handling](https://www.sqlite.org/conflict.html).

- **W10 evidence rule; W8 #4; W10 #5/#7 — disputed gold remains possible:** `tar -tvf` proves names/sizes, not equality with live bytes. Link count 2 does not prove the two displayed paths share an inode. Git `M` can represent mode changes rather than byte changes, and staged changes also matter. **Fix:** require archive/live content hashes, generated pairwise hard-link identity evidence, and complete Git status plus content evidence. Explicitly permit these commands in `fixtures-common.md`.

- **heldout-common.md hardness/balance — shallow shortcuts survive:** `hops` is self-declared; 30 lines can be irrelevant padding; harmless token duplication leaves positional cues intact. One safer-looking/worse pair still permits seven conventional cues. **Fix:** require a short artifact dependency explanation per pair, audit whether omitted artifacts change answerability, and counterbalance protective-token placement across mechanisms.

- **heldout-common.md feasibility — conflicting constraints and compressed scope:** a deciding wrapper capped at 25 lines cannot itself satisfy the 30-line deciding-artifact requirement. Eight pairs, six mechanisms, evidence, balancing and tests leave little debugging time within an hour, especially with Docker forbidden. **Fix:** exempt scripts from the 30-line rule, provide shared test scaffolding, and commission six pairs per worker first—18 is already within PLAN.md’s scope—then expand after Linux verification.

Read-only review; no files changed. Target-version execution was not performed.

**Verdict: revise the briefs before dispatch.**"""FS-2: deleting a link differs from deleting its target directory."""
import io
import tarfile
from pathlib import Path

from fixtures.base import FIXED_EPOCH, Artifact, Pair, Resource, Variant, file_fp, sh


CSV_2024 = b"year,total\n2024,12\n"
CSV_2025 = b"year,total\n2025,15\n"


def _build(root: Path, archive_in_uploads: bool) -> None:
    for directory in ("uploads", "store", "exports", "src"):
        (root / directory).mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    if archive_in_uploads:
        archive_dir = root / "uploads/archive"
        archive_dir.mkdir()
        (root / "store/archive").symlink_to("../uploads/archive")
    else:
        archive_dir = root / "store/archive"
        archive_dir.mkdir()
        (root / "uploads/archive").symlink_to("../store/archive")
    (archive_dir / "2024.csv").write_bytes(CSV_2024)
    (archive_dir / "2025.csv").write_bytes(CSV_2025)
    (root / "uploads/avatar.png").write_bytes(b"PNG sample bytes\n")
    (root / "uploads/tmp.part").write_bytes(b"partial upload\n")
    (root / "src/main.py").write_text("print('ready')\n")
    (root / "README.md").write_text("File storage notes.\n")
    (root / "src/settings.ini").write_text("mode=local\n")
    with tarfile.open(root / "exports/archive.tar", "w") as archive:
        info = tarfile.TarInfo("store/archive/2024.csv")
        info.size = len(CSV_2024)
        info.mtime = FIXED_EPOCH
        info.uid = info.gid = 0
        info.uname = info.gname = ""
        info.mode = 0o644
        archive.addfile(info, io.BytesIO(CSV_2024))


def _artifacts(root: Path):
    commands = (
        "ls -la uploads",
        "ls -la store",
        "tar -tf exports/archive.tar",
        "sha256sum store/archive/2024.csv store/archive/2025.csv",
        "tar -xOf exports/archive.tar store/archive/2024.csv | sha256sum",
        "ls -la",
    )
    return [Artifact("$ " + command, sh(root, command).stdout) for command in commands]


