codex
Implemented FS-5 through FS-10 and the held-out filesystem tests. No commit or package installation.

| Pair | Changed fact and trap | A: lost / recoverable | B: lost / recoverable | Hops; differing artifact lines |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| FS-5 | Grouping makes `find -delete` apply to both name branches instead of only `.gz`. | R3,R6 / R6 | R2,R3,R5,R6 / R5,R6 | 3; 1 |
| FS-6 | The ledger edit is unstaged versus staged; `checkout -- .` reads the index. | R5 / none | none / none | 2; 2 |
| FS-7 | A staged new file is removed by `reset --hard`; an untracked one survives. The archive has different bytes. | R7 / none | none / none | 2; 6 |
| FS-8 | `sed -i` replaces the inode; streaming output writes through the hard link. | R2 / R2 | R2 / none | 3; 1 |
| FS-9 | `dotglob` includes hidden cache entries in `cache/*`. | R1,R4,R5 / R4 | R1,R2,R4,R5,R6 / R2,R4 | 2; 1 |
| FS-10 | `stage` is a regular file versus a directory; only the directory receives `stage/src`. | R1,R2,R4,R6,R7 / none | R1,R2,R4,R6,R7 / all five | 2; 1 |

The counted artifacts supply these facts: FS-5 uses the log listing, wrapper expression, and live/archive hashes; FS-6 uses status columns and Git diffs; FS-7 uses status columns and live/archive hashes; FS-8 uses the wrapper, mode configuration, and generated same-file evidence; FS-9 uses the cache listing and wrapper option; FS-10 uses the destination type listing and wrapper. Confidence in the executed local gold is high for all six.

**Verification:** `.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q tests/test_fixtures_fs_heldout.py` → **7 passed**, no skips. This executed every operation and restore script on macOS. GNU/bookworm verification remains for the orchestrator, as required by the shared brief.

Files changed: [fs5.py](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/fs/fs5.py>), [fs6.py](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/fs/fs6.py>), [fs7.py](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/fs/fs7.py>), [fs8.py](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/fs/fs8.py>), [fs9.py](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/fs/fs9.py>), [fs10.py](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/fs/fs10.py>), and [test_fixtures_fs_heldout.py](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/tests/test_fixtures_fs_heldout.py>).

**Brief conflict:** The requirement for two pairs with a zero-loss variant necessarily repeats the empty lost-ID set, despite the rule against repeated lost-ID sets. The tests enforce uniqueness for nonempty sets across pairs. Nothing else was left undone locally.
